#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "8cb590c5-586d-56a1-af70-d693ea055419" :question/math-academy-id "e-14996" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) =\\sin{t} \\,\\mathbf i -\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}‖i+j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)}{1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/id #uuid "2f92f568-0bf5-58bd-87ce-e6be8d05fe69" :question/math-academy-id "e-14997" :question/problem "Given  the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\sqrt 3 t , \\:  \\cos t, \\: \\sin{t}\\right\\rangle,$ find the principal normal vector $\\mathbf N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve." :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\cdot N(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f5e6498b-73f9-5760-81bb-14e9acc495f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14998" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) = \\sin{t} \\cdot \\mathbf q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j+k)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-2\\,k) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6774fef1-a23b-59a7-859b-0334493c8db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358109} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e02ba9-8a36-54ea-9a29-e2d6978a66b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358110} {:answer/id #uuid "e0160c2c-74b1-5aa1-8d57-4c84b7be07f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358111} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b92453-93e0-583d-8fe6-c805203adcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358112} {:answer/id #uuid "59951837-d4a6-52a1-baa1-6be583338b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0160c2c-74b1-5aa1-8d57-4c84b7be07f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358111} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d84f76-9265-5f0f-9224-db03e41a7003" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9ec2fe9-559e-547b-851b-6351eb71769b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127333" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}⟨1,\\,2t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}⟨1,\\,2t⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}})⟨1,\\,2t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{4t}{(4{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,2t⟩+\\frac{1}{(4{t}^{2}+1)^{1/2}}⟨0,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(4{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}[-4t⟨1,\\,2t⟩+(4{t}^{2}+1)⟨0,\\,2⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(4{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨-4t,\\,2\\,⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{(4(1)^{2}+1)^{3/2}}⟨-4(1),\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{5}^{3}}}⟨-4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}‖⟨-2,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩}{(\\frac{2}{5})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1)\\cdot T(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1⟩\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,\\,2⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(⟨-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-2)(1)+(1)(2)) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9f83511-f8f8-5e86-a05b-42141d573e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358116} {:answer/id #uuid "20f9876a-c1a7-56d7-8f6c-168e11afd0b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-1,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358117} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab67ef1-9cc8-5f15-9030-652cb2cb3a4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-\\sqrt{3},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358118} {:answer/id #uuid "167371ad-e636-5d0c-a8e3-c28b926fcaa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358119} {:answer/id #uuid "b928e174-080e-5370-8e53-ae9e1b638248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9f83511-f8f8-5e86-a05b-42141d573e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358116} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e54b48b-69e5-5f5d-b9b0-fa8a2f5d9fb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a07d77c9-e218-56b9-bd65-eec5d18def38" :question/math-academy-id "q-127335" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖ & =‖⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}((-\\sqrt{3})(-1)+(1)(-\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62d8ffc6-1b65-5186-92c7-8b69eb44186b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(i+j-2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358123} {:answer/id #uuid "be4bb81d-0425-50e1-9b80-c3700abd7096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358124} {:answer/id #uuid "de6bfed1-d359-5576-b84e-0c4ab1cdaa85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(2\\,i-j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358125} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6642c2-10d8-5c31-9bd8-ff2025660821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(-2\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358126} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf2b02b-ae0a-5cf9-bb35-d3a3da690655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be4bb81d-0425-50e1-9b80-c3700abd7096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358124} :answer-field/id #uuid "69eec131-0c98-5119-bb9f-2257c730ab1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c57537a4-6650-5b6e-a37e-dea399e9f547" :question/math-academy-id "q-127577" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(1)=-i+j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\,{q}^{′}(1)=2\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{2(1)}{({1}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\cdot q(1)+\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+1}\\cdot {q}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-i+j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k)+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =|\\frac{1}{2}|\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-i+j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-1+1+0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb236e5f-3947-5082-ac3b-ddc972cc11a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358130} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c4f843-541c-5152-869b-9c5475da6410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358131} {:answer/id #uuid "94ab151b-08ae-5ab3-9c6e-fbcf2048794b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358132} {:answer/id #uuid "534dbe77-fe0f-52e6-8a51-0798587dbc9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358133} {:answer/id #uuid "81cba0b9-9846-5fc5-8cba-df2f52c161de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81cba0b9-9846-5fc5-8cba-df2f52c161de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358134} :answer-field/id #uuid "e074691c-7fd1-53e0-9c10-4838926f6f8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf9f85ef-5964-5687-a67a-ae8292a29c88" :question/math-academy-id "q-127595" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=2,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(2)=⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(2)=⟨0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(1+4t)^{3/2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{2}{(1+4(2))^{3/2}}\\cdot q(2)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4(2)}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩+\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}(-2⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩+9⟨0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}⟨-2,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{54}⟨-4,\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\frac{1}{54}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{54}\\cdot \\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{54}⟨-4,\\sqrt{2}⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{18})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨-4,\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2)\\cdot N(2) & =\\frac{1}{3}q(2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(1\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2})+2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(-2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a46ef59a-b3d7-57f4-99e9-aea45bb2de86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358137} {:answer/id #uuid "de15ae0d-d665-5c65-81a2-b877b6189aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358138} {:answer/id #uuid "aa55d302-6d8e-555e-8739-5ea82b35a919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358139} {:answer/id #uuid "4768a721-f8cc-558e-b299-d88efb82f886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{2},1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358140} {:answer/id #uuid "7da246ac-177c-57ca-9ae3-d47db683575b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,0,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa55d302-6d8e-555e-8739-5ea82b35a919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358139} :answer-field/id #uuid "e75c62d3-4fc3-5b2b-b7e8-83f2f335a0fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "811d16c6-f719-59c2-bae0-963973f1dd90" :question/math-academy-id "q-127598" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=⟨1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{({e}^{t}+1)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{({e}^{0}+1)^{2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{{e}^{0}+1}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}q(0)+\\frac{1}{2}{q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩+\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩+2⟨1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0,-1⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(1(1)+\\sqrt{2}(0)+1(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(1+0-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2497e075-dac7-5e11-a04a-0e515c8fb121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+\\,j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358144} {:answer/id #uuid "ea245ad7-c64b-5fe7-a0ae-93eac4eeaee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(2\\,i-\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358145} {:answer/id #uuid "75767924-b27a-5cd6-829c-2ef6db3f49ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358146} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd662c5-936f-51e4-bcf0-a0f12b06909c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(-2\\,i+\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358147} {:answer/id #uuid "c6583431-c4e4-57c4-a53a-fb5013acde80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i-2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75767924-b27a-5cd6-829c-2ef6db3f49ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358146} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8653fd2-8d48-5c85-aea6-f6f5f949c3f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d57e43b-681d-5aa1-a53c-acdd85847d41" :question/math-academy-id "q-127600" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=-2\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2(0)}{({0}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =0(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)+\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-j+k}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-j\\cdot j+k\\cdot k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-1+1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d65e014-21a9-5274-9af8-68fd0b6e181d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d65e014-21a9-5274-9af8-68fd0b6e181d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358150} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc2f2647-0f41-5cb3-bf5b-441d67ee36f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbe7871a-ccc9-5a95-8370-21299423ec38" :question/math-academy-id "q-222851" :question/problem "Given  the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93d35037-2355-54a0-bd11-31bcdd84666d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93d35037-2355-54a0-bd11-31bcdd84666d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358153} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a1c5cd-19e6-50e8-b751-e379afec2c52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57ae3ae2-5537-5a6c-8ff4-29e7e6b5713a" :question/math-academy-id "q-223101" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$k$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k)}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34b83896-612a-5049-863f-b0ed764ddff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t)+\\cos(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358156} {:answer/id #uuid "70294066-ba5d-59c8-af12-cf18ed7073c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t)-\\cos(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70294066-ba5d-59c8-af12-cf18ed7073c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t)-\\cos(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358157} :answer-field/id #uuid "43f40e9f-a746-51b7-9de6-9cc9ca7e2132" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2a00aaf-4fea-576d-a595-b5a4e77ae597" :question/math-academy-id "q-223102" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =|-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|‖⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{2({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35d1aab3-dc12-5a5d-bd05-2e15a3be1e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358160} {:answer/id #uuid "84a43804-88b1-59cf-8612-ea88c69d49d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35d1aab3-dc12-5a5d-bd05-2e15a3be1e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358160} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f448f39-df0b-5383-9c83-cd75facb6224" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da013da8-eeeb-5c15-a659-cf12ca1a50f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-223107" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,k$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n$j$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}\\,t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k \\\\  \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\  \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a874d8e3-9a22-519e-9812-7190479ca678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}i - \\frac{4}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117571} {:answer/id #uuid "c84f8d58-bc46-54b6-a742-c27b8da8ade3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}j - \\frac{4}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117572} {:answer/id #uuid "e2170b0a-e694-5929-96ce-1fe3794d1111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}j - \\frac{3}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117573} {:answer/id #uuid "52575909-820a-5276-b2d2-7400ea1affec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}i + \\frac{4}{5}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117574} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb4c81a-b3c7-5508-91d2-d81d952a3208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}i - \\frac{3}{5}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c84f8d58-bc46-54b6-a742-c27b8da8ade3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}j - \\frac{4}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117572} :answer-field/id #uuid "a053211b-e5f5-5c7e-af98-df1f8ec16472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fee02912-87a3-5a99-92ba-f8ae9f270c88" :question/math-academy-id "q-33233" :question/problem "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(0,3,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ) \\\\ T(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,3,4)$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,3,4⟩ \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+3\\text{ }j+4\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components of $i$, $j$, and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=3 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,3,4)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k}{1}=-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(-i)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(i\\cdot j)+\\frac{4}{5}(i\\cdot k) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "245b1417-c3cc-59a3-b7e5-84c910bb65f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358164} {:answer/id #uuid "965792b3-c480-50a9-a3fe-465b0c1803ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358165} {:answer/id #uuid "6c24731d-bed8-5eb3-ae35-83f231614beb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358166} {:answer/id #uuid "8b25f59c-0d43-5594-a2a5-be3e0c189b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358167} {:answer/id #uuid "07900b3e-f73e-5611-a64d-f640c76f2837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "245b1417-c3cc-59a3-b7e5-84c910bb65f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358164} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddca4876-5cbb-5d8b-9b52-6eb0a651b82f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "753967dd-6567-58b2-be47-fe7de36dbf79" :question/math-academy-id "q-33525" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨1,t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨1,t⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}})⟨1,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨1,t⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}[-t⟨1,t⟩+(1+{t}^{2})⟨0,1⟩] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-t,1⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1+{1}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}‖⟨-1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,1⟩}{\\frac{1}{2}}=⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$  we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "269bd28e-a943-5339-b349-855b7404a2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cos(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "269bd28e-a943-5339-b349-855b7404a2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cos(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358171} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf670fa-a625-5e05-bc16-e4de23407224" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff5929d5-de16-5f0f-a635-8dcfd92d6614" :question/math-academy-id "q-358782" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,12t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,12⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25(1)+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,12⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{12}{13}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{12}{13})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{13}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{169}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{5}{13}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{5}{13})}=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ced17c8-d940-57ca-8c4f-6fbc5f9c67a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358174} {:answer/id #uuid "0a070b1e-d089-5c41-bfd7-90d2c71ad071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358175} {:answer/id #uuid "170eb424-1d67-54f4-8508-d42361f4226b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358176} {:answer/id #uuid "19077fad-f24d-5334-861c-526ce77e9a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358177} {:answer/id #uuid "8bbecc7b-388c-578a-8742-154932d486f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "170eb424-1d67-54f4-8508-d42361f4226b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358176} :answer-field/id #uuid "746551b8-f465-5c34-aec7-2999622eda4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cdc8f89-20af-5035-804f-26faa19aaac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-74851" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨t,4⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨t,4⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}})\\cdot ⟨t,4⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨t,4⟩) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+16)\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨t,4⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{0}{({0}^{2}+16)\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨0,4⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+16}}\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{4}‖⟨1,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0⟩}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0+0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6c8dc48-3bb5-5912-ab67-fec2dea050fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358181} {:answer/id #uuid "ec11643d-fc6d-5a1c-96cc-321e91ca2e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358182} {:answer/id #uuid "940168b7-6654-5404-a929-a6c153fb5c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358183} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d09f6f-970c-51fa-bb18-39fae1b738b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358184} {:answer/id #uuid "555ed1f1-1a7f-5c7a-89e5-1ee19a8cc3b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "940168b7-6654-5404-a929-a6c153fb5c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358183} :answer-field/id #uuid "764d2749-e15a-5707-950a-96d1f14325a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8c3f8ef-4d49-5c32-a539-c6eef2f1ab59" :question/math-academy-id "q-75491" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨3,0,-4⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{5}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
